[THOUGHT] The patient cannot specify wound location — a critical clinical detail. Since physical examination was previously requested but only partial findings were returned (e.g., pulses, edema, Doppler, skin discoloration), the missing elements — wound dimensions, tissue type, exudate, and *precise anatomical location* — remain essential to confirm classic venous ulcer morphology. Among these, location is highly discriminating: >70% of venous ulcers occur at the medial malleolus due to valvular incompetence in the saphenofemoral junction and gravitational stasis. Without knowing location, we risk misclassifying atypical ulcers (e.g., lateral malleolus — more associated with trauma or arterial disease; dorsal foot — neuropathic). Therefore, the next highest-yield action is to complete the physical exam — specifically requesting the wound’s anatomical site and visual characteristics — as this can be obtained immediately at bedside and does not require lab/imaging turnaround.

[TEST] Physical examination of the wound to document:  
- Anatomical location (e.g., medial malleolus, lateral malleolus, anterior shin, dorsum of foot);  
- Dimensions (length × width × depth in cm);  
- Tissue type (e.g., granulation tissue, slough, necrotic/eschar, epithelialization);  
- Exudate (none, scant, moderate, copious; serous, serosanguineous, purulent);  
- Presence of erythema, induration, or undermining.

[THOUGHT] The biopsy result has not been provided — likely because it is pending or not yet available in the current data set. At this point, we have strong convergent evidence for chronic venous insufficiency as the underlying cause of the non-healing leg ulcer:  
- Objective Doppler confirmation of venous reflux and obstruction;  
- Clinical stigmata: varicose veins, asymmetric edema (worse on right), hyperpigmented periwound skin (stasis dermatitis);  
- Diminished but palpable arterial pulses — ruling out critical ischemia;  
- Six-month duration — consistent with chronic venous ulcer progression.  
While ideal to confirm with wound location and biopsy, the pretest probability of chronic venous ulcer is now very high (>90%) given this constellation. Other diagnoses (e.g., diabetic neuropathic ulcer) lack supporting history (no mention of diabetes, neuropathy, or foot deformity); arterial ulcer is unlikely with preserved pulses; pyoderma gangrenosum typically presents with painful, rapidly expanding ulcers with violaceous borders — not described; malignancy would require biopsy confirmation but is a lower-probability event *de novo* without reported changes like bleeding, nodularity, or rapid growth. Per diagnostic efficiency and turn constraints, we have sufficient evidence to make the most likely diagnosis.

[DIAGNOSIS] Chronic venous ulcer

